  "notes": [
    "carBox/wheels/segModel: the cream/burgundy car sits on cream/burgundy Godzilla card art, so the lite segmenter cut away most of the body; segModel birefnet-general, carBox re-measured on a 4x ruler crop (wing tip x~116 to nose x~394, roof y~450 to tyre bottoms y~540) and padded a few px, which was the box that kept the whole rear bumper in a side-by-side segmenter test; wheel circles measured on the same crop. The wing endplate (dark burgundy on burgundy art) is still not cut out",
    "model.glass: the sheet's window masks, all five views hand-traced (replace) on ruler crops of the drawing. The automatic finder took the rear wing's Japanese lettering, the cream/burgundy split line on the sides and the hood/cowl line for glass and caught only blobs of the dark-tinted windscreen and rear window on the top view. Side views: rear quarter + door glass either side of the B-pillar; rear view: three panes either side of the two wing uprights (the uprights stay opaque).",
    "model.rebake.glass: windows traced on the Tripo mesh itself (tripo-sheet.png paint + tripo-clay.png panel edges, ruler crops). Tripo modelled the side windows as open holes with seats behind, so side/left are left out (nothing to paint; 'sheet' there also put magenta on the beltline). Top: Tripo's rear window sits ~40-60 px aft of the drawing's, so it is traced on the mesh panel (x 224-348; the black band x 190-224 behind it stays opaque, like a frit), and the windscreen from the roof edge x~556 to the cowl x~655, inside the A-pillars; one half traced past the centre line and mirrored (stopping at the centre left a 1-px painted seam down both windows). Front: below the roof edge (y 1167) to the cowl (y 1241), inside the A-pillar, mirrored. Rear view deliberately not listed: the raked rear window is seen best from the top anyway, and a rear-view outline also made the wing uprights' side faces glass at grazing angles; the top outline alone covers the whole pane and leaves the uprights, claw-mark roof and wing lettering opaque.",
    "model.fit width 0.975: clean's size check said width 1.003 against the top drawing, but the silhouettes showed Tripo's usual extra width - cyan bands both sides on silhouette-front/rear (max width 1.046 front, 1.048 rear) and at the front fender corners on silhouette-top (max 1.042). 0.975 splits it: max widths come to ~1.02 while the mid-body stays within 3% of the top drawing",
    "model.fit height 0.99: after the width fix promote measured height 1.053 vs the side drawing (clean had 1.042, already near the limit); silhouette-side shows the model's front splitter and rear valance hanging below the drawn ones (cyan under both bumpers). 0.99 brings it inside 5%; 0.98 overshot and dropped side IoU below its 0.93 gate",
    "mask.add / mask.remove (photo px, traced on a 5x ruler crop): the segmenter can't tell the car's burgundy tail from the matching Godzilla card art, so the rear wing (x 115-147, y 453-472) and the lower rear quarter/bumper behind the rear wheel (x 112-150, y 476-526) are drawn in by hand; the underside between the wheels is cut back to the sills (y < 531), the mask ran ~3 px into the blister there (founder)",
    "model.rebake.noalign top: its alignment was weak (match 0.26; Tripo never saw the top and painted the roof plain red) and sheared the roof claw marks into broken, stepped pieces (founder: 'the godzilla scratch is a little jagged'); projected straight through the camera instead. Tried rear too: it doubled the GO-JIRA plate and badge, so the rear stays aligned",
    "model.rebake.flat: the wing (top x 46-122 y 790-1048, rear face x 605-905 y 1133-1175): Tripo embossed its own invented lettering into the wing's normal map under the drawn ホットウィール; flattened so only the drawn lettering shows (founder: the rear wing should look like the renders)",
    "model.rebake.trust top + skip rear, on the wing: the rear drawing is shot from above, so its ホットウィール is the lettering on the wing's TOP; projected from behind it smeared garbled dark letters across the wing top's front edge. The rear drawing never paints the wing; the top drawing (which sees it squarely) always wins there"
  ],
